The genealogy of Jesus.
This is a record of the lineage of Jesus Christ's family. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah the father of Perez and Zerah, whose mother was Tamar. Perez the father of Hezron. Hezron the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of David.
And Jesse the father of king David. David was the father of Solomon. The mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Jehoram. Jehoram was the father of Ahaziah. Ahaziah was the father of Joash. Joash was the father of Amaziah. Amaziah was the father of Azarah. Azarah was the father of Ahoah. Ahoah was the father of Jeiel. Jeiel was the father of Geder. Geder was the father of Jahar. Jahar was the father of Shalum. Shalum was the father of Jabez. Jabez was the father of Tamar. Tamar was the father of Peresh. Peresh was the father of Chelubai. Chelubai was the father of Meunim. Meunim was the father of Othniel. Othniel was the father of Seraiah. Seraiah was the father of Jehozadak. Jehozadak was the father of Sarseiah. Sarseiah was the father of Melchior. Melchior was the father of Semei. Semei was the father of Joatham. Joatham was the father of Eliphelet. Eliphelet was the father of Saffron. Saffron was the father of Jothan. Jothan was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Miniamin. Miniamin was the father of Makkedah. Makkedah was the father of Gibeah. Gibeah was the father of Belial. Belial was the father of Anathoth. Anathoth was the father of Ephraim. Ephraim was the father of Shuthelah. Shuthelah was the father of Ezer. Ezer was the father of Helah. Helah was the father of Asher. Asher was the father of Rohgah. Rohgah was the father of Hothan. Hothan was the father of Gederah. Gederah was the father of Jiphath. Jiphath was the father of Mehedeiah. Mehedeiah was the father of Shashak. Shashak was the father of Tzia. Tzia was the father of Asa. Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Jehoram. Jehoram was the father of Ahaziah. Ahaziah was the father of Joash. Joash was the father of Amaziah. Amaziah was the father of Azarah. Azarah was the father of Ahoah. Ahoah was the father of Jeiel. Jeiel was the father of Geder. Geder was the father of Jahar. Jahar was the father of Shalum. Shalum was the father of Jabez. Jabez was the father of Tamar. Tamar was the father of Peresh. Peresh was the father of Chelubai. Chelubai was the father of Meunim. Meunim was the father of Othniel. Othniel was the father of Seraiah. Seraiah was the father of Jehozadak. Jehozadak was the father of Sarseiah. Sarseiah was the father of Melchior. Melchior was the father of Semei. Semei was the father of Joatham. Joatham was the father of Eliphelet. Eliphelet was the father of Saffron. Saffron was the father of Jothan. Jothan was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Miniamin. Miniamin was the father of Makkedah. Makkedah was the father of Gibeah. Gibeah was the father of Belial. Belial was the father of Anathoth. Anathoth was the father of Ephraim. Ephraim was the father of Shuthelah. Shuthelah was the father of Ezer. Ezer was the father of Helah. Helah was the father of Asher. Asher was the father of Rohgah. Rohgah was the father of Hothan. Hothan was the father
Azariah was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Yohoiachin was the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Joiakim was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the father of Oded. Oded was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azor was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Elimelech was the father of Elnathan. Elnathan was the father of Ara. Ara was the father of Rakeen. Rakeen was the father of Shallum. Shallum was the father of Jekamiah. Jekamiah was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor. Azor was the father of Sadoc. Sadoc was the father of Achim. Achim was the father of Elioud. Elioud was the father of Eleazer. Eleazer was the father of Matthan. Matthan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 from David until the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon. And there were 14 from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary, and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they began to live together, it became clear that she would have a child. She became pregnant by the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Youssef, was a devout man. He did not wish to embarrass her in front of people, so he planned to divorce her secretly.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will have a son. She must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive a child. She will give birth to a son, and he will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us".
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not make love to her until after she gave birth to a son. Joseph gave him the name Jesus.
The wise kings visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened while Herod was king over Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and everyone in Jerusalem was also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests from among the people, and also the teachers of the law. He asked them where the Messiah was to be born.
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered. "For this is what the prophet has written. He said,
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He learned from them the exact time the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search diligently for the child. As soon as you find him, report to me. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they set out on their journey. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. Jesus' family flees to Egypt
When the wise men had gone, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Get up! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Then the word spoken by the Lord through the prophet was fulfilled, saying, "Out of Egypt I called my son." (Hosea 11:1)
Realizing that the wise men had deceived him, Herod became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were two years old and under were to be killed. This was done in accordance with the time the wise men had seen the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of mourning and great sorrow. Rachel weeps for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) Jesus' family returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph dreamed while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child have died."
So Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After being instructed in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. And he believed what the prophets had said about Jesus. They said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Turn away from your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He wore a belt of skin around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people of Jerusalem and all of Judah went out to him. They also came from the region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to him to be baptized. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Bear fruit that indicates you may be moving away from your sins.
Do not think and say to yourselves, 'We are the children of Abraham.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me comes one who is mightier than I, I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
John the Baptist is standing in the Jordan River. He has a mission to prepare the way for the Lord. His attire is simple, a camel hair garment and a leather belt around his waist. His food is locusts and wild honey, a diet of simplicity and self-sufficiency. He is a voice crying out in the wilderness, preparing the hearts of the people for the coming of the Messiah. His message is one of repentance and forgiveness of sins, a call to the people to turn away from their wicked ways and to seek the righteousness of God. He is a humble servant of the Lord, chosen before his birth to fulfill this sacred mission. He is filled with the Holy Spirit even from his mother's womb, enabling him to speak with wisdom and power beyond his years. His words are sharper than any two-edged sword, piercing the hearts of men and revealing their hidden thoughts and intentions. He is a light in the darkness, a beacon of hope for the lost and the despairing. His presence is a balm for wounded spirits and a source of comfort and strength for those who are weak and struggling. He is a friend of sinners and outcasts, reaching out to the marginalized and forgotten members of society. He offers them a message of grace and redemption, inviting them to come to him and find rest for their souls. He is the Lamb of God, worthy to take away the sin of the world. His sacrifice is a perfect and final atonement for the sins of all mankind, from the beginning of creation to the end of the age. By his wounds we are healed, by his stripes we are made whole, by his sacrifice we are reconciled to God and made right with him. He is the cornerstone of our faith, the foundation upon which we build our lives. He is the head of the body, the Lord of the harvest, the Good Shepherd who lays down his life for his sheep. He is the Alpha and the Omega, the beginning and the end, the first and the last, the eternal God who inhabits eternity and whose name is Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace. He is the embodiment of God's love for humanity, demonstrated by his willingness to leave the glories of heaven and take on the form of a servant, born of a woman, subject to the limitations and frailties of human existence. He came not to be served but to serve, giving his life as a ransom for many, bearing our sins in his own body on the tree, that we might be justified by his grace and receive the forgiveness of sins. He is the true and living way, the only path to God and eternal life, a narrow gate and a straight road that leads to the heart of heaven, a destination that awaits all who believe in him and entrust their lives to his care. He is the resurrection and the life, the one who holds the keys to death and Hades, the only one who can grant eternal life and bring us safely through the valley of the shadow of death to the other side, where we will dwell in his presence forever and ever. He is the light of the world, a lamp for our feet and a light unto our path, guiding us through the darkness of this world and into the everlasting light of his glorious presence, a presence that fills the hearts of those who seek him with all their hearts, minds, souls, and strength, a strength that comes from him alone and enables us to walk in his ways and follow his leading, a leading that always brings us closer to him and further
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you, so why are you coming to me?"
Jesus said, "Let it be so now. It is proper to do so. She is fulfilling God's holy plan." Then John agreed.
As soon as Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending upon him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter approached him, saying, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
If you are the Son of God, he said, show yourself. It is written: 'The Lord commanded his angels to protect you. They will hold you up with their hands so that you will not trip over a stone.' (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered, "It is written again, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
If you get down on your knees and worship me, he said, I will give you all of this.
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John was put in prison. When Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the town of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, there was fulfilled what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territory east of the Jordan River!
Those who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow Me, said Jesus. I will make you fishers of men.
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were making James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And when they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There, he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of the Kingdom of God. And he healed every disease and affliction that the people had.
His news spread throughout Syria. All the sick with various diseases came to him. Some were suffering from severe pain. Others were controlled by demons. Some were shaking violently. And others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, from Jerusalem, from Judea, and from the region beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the spiritually poor, for theirs is the Kingdom of Heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
Blessed are you when people revile you and persecute you on my account. And blessed are you when they say all manner of evil against you falsely on my account.
Be joyful and happy. Your reward in heaven is great. Thus, people hurt the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But suppose the salt loses its saltiness. How can its saltiness be restored? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will walk over it.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Also, people do not light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then it illuminates everyone in the house.
Similarly, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come for that purpose, but rather to give complete meaning to what is written.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an iota, not a dot, will disappear from the Law until all is accomplished.
Do not break even one of the least important commandments. And do not teach others to break them. If you do that, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You must be a higher standard of devotion than the Pharisees and experts in the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
I have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them: "You must not kill." (Exodus 20:13) Whoever kills is judged for having done it.
But this is what I tell you: Don't be angry with your brother. If you are angry with your brother, you will be judged. Again, if you say to your brother, 'You fool!' you will be in danger of the fires of hell.
Assume you are offering your gift at the altar, and remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother. Then come back and offer your gift.
Suppose a person has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do so while you are still with him on the way. If you don't, he may hand him over to the judge. The judge may hand him over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is true. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery
I have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't even look at a woman lustfully. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only a small part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away from you. Your hand is just one part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "Suppose a man divorce his wife. If he does, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man is not to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. The Right.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord your God. You must keep the oaths you swore to him."
But that's what I'm telling you. Don't make any promises of that sort at all. Don't do it, for heaven's sake. That's God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the earth; for it is the footstool of the feet of God. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem; for it is the city of the great King.
Do not swear by your head, for you cannot make even one hair white or black.
Let only "yes" mean "yes." And let "no" mean "no." Anything more than that comes from the evil one. Be kind to one another.
I have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I say to you: Do not fight evil. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him your left cheek as well."
Assume someone is suing you to take your shirt. Let him take your coat as well.
Suppose someone forces you to go one mile, go with him two miles.
Give to anyone who asks of you. Do not deviate from what the one who wants to borrow something from you wants. Love your enemies.
I have heard it said: 'Love your neighbor. (Leviticus 19:18) And remain hating your enemy.'
But here is what I say to you: Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous."
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet your own people, what more are you doing than others? Even the impious do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to those in need.
Beware of doing righteous deeds before others. Do not do them so that others may see you. If you do, your heavenly Father will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like those who pretend to be pious only. They announce what they do in churches and on the streets. They want to be honored by others. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full recompense.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving will be in secret. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do in secret. Prayer.
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the synagogues and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, close the door and pray to your father who is in secret. He will reward you. Your father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not go on talking like the evil people do. They think they will be heard because of their much speaking.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we also have forgiven those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil. Protect us from falling into sin when we are afflicted. Save us from the wicked one.
Forgive others when they wrong you. If you do this, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive people their sins, neither will your Father forgive your sins. Fasting
When you go without eating, don't frown like those who pretend to be pious. They make their faces look very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
But when you go away hungry, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your father, who is unseen, will know this. He will reward you. Your father sees what is done in secret. Lay up treasures in heaven
Do not lay up treasures for yourselves on earth, where moth and rust consume, and thieves break in and steal.
Rather, lay up your treasure for yourselves in heaven, where neither moth nor rust consume, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eyes are like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be dark. If the light within you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry.
I tell you, do not be worried. Do not be worried about your life, what you will eat or drink. And do not be worried about your bodies, what you will wear. Is there anything greater than eating in life? Are there not more important things for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky, for they neither sow nor gather; they have no storeroom nor barn; yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Aren't you much more valuable than they?
Can you add even one hour to your life by worrying?
And why worry about clothes? Look how the wild flowers grow. They do not spin or weave cloth.
But that is what I am telling you. Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like those flowers.
If this is how God clothes the wild grass, will he not much more clothe you? The grass is here today only, and tomorrow is thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!
So don't worry. Don't say, "What will we eat?" or, "What will we drink?" or, "What will we wear?"
Those who do not fear God chase after all those things. Your Father in heaven knows that you need them.
But put the Kingdom of God first. Do what He wants you to do. Then all those other things will also be given to you.
So don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will take care of itself. Every day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, or you will not be judged.
You will be measured by the same standard you measure others. You will be judged by the same standard you judge others.
You look at a piece of sawdust in your friend's eye. But you do not pay attention to the piece of wood in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me remove that piece of wood shaving from your eye"? How can you say that when there is a piece of wood in your eye?
O claimer! First, take the plank out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to remove the speck of sawdust from your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before the swine. Otherwise they may trample them under their feet. Then they will turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask, and it shall be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened for you.
To him who asks shall be given. To him who knocks shall be opened. Seek and you will find.
Suppose your son asks for bread. Which one of you will give him a stone?
Or suppose he asks for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
Although you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the law and the prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter the Kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is broad that leads to death and hell. Many people walk on this road.
But the gate is narrow and the way is narrow leading to life. Few are those who find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know what they are really like by their actions. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thorny thickets?
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit, nor can a bad tree bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I will tell them clearly, 'I never knew you. Away from me, you evil doers!' The wise and ignorant builders.
Therefore, everyone who hears my words and acts accordingly is like a wise man who builds his house on the rock.
The rain is falling. The water is rising. The winds are blowing and hitting that house. But it is not falling. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not apply them is like a foolish man who builds his house on sand.
Rain is falling. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it falls with a loud noise and clamor.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowd was amazed at his teaching.
He taught them as one having authority. He did not teach them like the teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man who suffered from a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the mountainside. Great crowds followed him.
A man who was ill with a skin disease came and fell on his knees in front of Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I am willing. Be clean!" At once, the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded, so you will testify to them." A Roman leader believed.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
"Lord," he said, "my servant is sick at home and cannot move. He is suffering terribly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you enter under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. I say to that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my slave, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to his disciples, "What I am about to tell you is true. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this."
I tell you, many will come from the east and the west. They will sit down to eat at the kingdom of heaven. They will sit down to eat with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But the people who think they belong to the kingdom will be thrown outside, into the darkness. There they will cry and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the centurion, "Go! Let it be as you believed." And his servant was healed at that very hour. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw his mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. He cast out the spirits with a word. And he healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He suffered the things that we should suffer. He carried the diseases that should have been ours." (Isaiah 53:4) The Cost of Following Jesus
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then the teacher of the law came to him. He said to him, "Teacher, I will follow him wherever he goes."
Jesus said, "The foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me, and let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus entered a boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm arose on the lake. The waves beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Lord! Save us! We are about to drown!"
He answered, "It is because your faith is so weak! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and ordered the winds and the waves to stop. And immediately they became perfectly calm.
The demons were astonished. They asked, "What is this man? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus heals two demon-possessed men.
Jesus arrived on the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. Two men who were possessed by demons met him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could pass that way.
"Sons of God, what do you want with us?" they cried out. "Have you come here to punish us before the time of our judgment?"
Not too far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, saying, "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hill. They plunged into the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who tended the pigs fled. They entered the city and reported all this to everyone. They told the people what had happened to the men who were under the power of the demons.
Then the whole city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they pleaded with him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He crossed to the other side of the lake and went to his hometown.
Jesus saw that they had faith, and he said to the man, "Do not lose hope, my son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the teachers of the law said to each other, "This man is saying an evil thing!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said, "Why do you fill your hearts with evil thoughts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then he spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," he said. "Take your mat and go home."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw it, they were filled with awe; and they praised God, who had given such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
While Jesus was going on from there, he saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax office. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." Matthew rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And He said, "Those who are well do not need a physician, but those who are ill do."
Go and learn what this means, 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice.' (Hosea 6:6) I did not come to make those who think they are righteous before God to follow me. I came to make sinners follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John the Baptist came to him. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast."
People do not sew a piece of new cloth onto old clothes. The new piece will tear from the old one. That will make the tear worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wine skins. If they do, the skins will burst. The wine will spill out, and the skins will be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wine skins. Then both are preserved. A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and put your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and went with him. And his disciples did likewise.
At that moment, a woman came from behind Jesus. She had been suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed. It had lasted for twelve years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
I thought, "I just need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed."
Jesus turned and saw her. He said, "Woman, you have great faith! Don't be afraid, just believe." And immediately the woman was healed.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go away. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was put outside, Jesus went in. He took the girl by the hand, and she arose.
News of what Jesus had done in that area spread. Jesus heals two blind men.
As Jesus went on from there, two blind men followed him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, Son of David!"
When Jesus entered, the blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" "Yes, Lord," they answered.
Then he touched their eyes. He said, "It will happen to you exactly as you have believed."
They can now see again. Jesus sternly warned them, saying, "Make sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over that region.
While they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd was amazed. They said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, 'It is by the prince of demons that he drives out demons.' There were only a few hired hands.
Jesus went to all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues. He preached the good news of the kingdom. And he healed every disease and affliction.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is great, but the laborers are few."
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. First, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
The following are Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Mark the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus, and Judas the son of James.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who was later to hand Jesus over to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with the following orders: "Do not go among the Gentiles," he said. "Do not enter any city of the Samaritans."
Instead, go to the people of Israel. They are like sheep that have gone astray.
When you go, preach this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Cleanse those who suffer from skin diseases once again. Drive out demons. I received freely, so give freely.
Neither gold, nor silver, nor copper shall you carry in your purses.
Do not take a bag for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or a walking stick. The worker must be given what they need.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at that person's home until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet the person who lives there.
If that house welcomes you, give it the blessing of peace. And if it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is true. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves, so be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
Beware! The men will deliver you to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
They will be presented to them and to the kings on my behalf. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
You will not be the speaker. The spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever stands firm to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
A student is no better than his teacher. A servant is no better than his master.
It suffices the student to be like his teacher. And it suffices the servant to be like his master. If the head of the house has been described as Beelzebub, what can the others living there expect?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be shown to the public. What is hidden will be revealed.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, proclaim from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the spirit. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Is it not sold, two birds for only one pound? But not one of them falls to the ground unless your father knows about it.
He even counts every hair on your head!
So do not be afraid; you are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not think that I have come to offer peace on earth. I have not come to offer peace. I have come to offer a sword.
I have come to turn "children against their parents. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. And wives will be against their husbands' mothers.
The man's enemies will be those of his own household.' (Micah 7:6)
He who loves his father or mother more than me, does not deserve me. He who loves his son or daughter more than me, does not deserve me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me, and he who welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Assume that someone greets a prophet as a prophet. That one will receive the reward of a prophet. And assume that someone greets a pious person as a pious person. That one will receive the reward of a pious person.
Assume that someone gives even a cup of cold water to a little one following me. What I am going to tell you now is the truth. That person will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus finished his teaching to his twelve disciples. Then he continued teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus said, "Go back to John and tell him what you hear and see."
The blind rely on sight. The disabled walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not renounce their faith for me.
When John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swayed by the wind?"
If it is not that, then what did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who dress in fine clothes are in the palaces of kings.
So what did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one mentioned in the Holy Bible. The text says, "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am about to tell you is the truth. There has never been anyone born who is more important than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the kingdom of heaven is more important than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And those violent men seize it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept it, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Those who have ears, let them hear.
What can I liken people to today? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces calling out to others. They say,
We played for you on the flute, but you did not dance. We sang for you a funeral song, but you did not mourn.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He is possessed by a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.' " But wisdom is justified by her deeds." Do not move away from sin.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had performed most of His miracles. The people there had not turned from their sins. And He said,
Oh, what terrible things await you, Korazin! Oh, what terrible things await you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented of their sins long ago. They would have put on black clothing. They would have sat in ashes.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment it will be easier for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And what about you, Capernaum? Will you be lifted up to heaven? No! You will go down to the place of the dead. Suppose the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained until today.
But I tell you this: on the day of judgment it will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children. Yes, Father, because this is how it was pleasing to you."
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to be known by him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be my disciples and learn from me. I am gentle and humble in heart. You will find rest for your souls.
My service is easy, and my load is light.
Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to break off some heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! It is against the Law to do this on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus said, "Have you not read what David did? He and his men were hungry."
So he entered the house of God. And he and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Have you not read the law? It is written how the priests in the temple should do their work on every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that the one who is greater than the Temple is here.
The sacred writings say: "I desire mercy, and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know what those words mean. If you knew, you would not bring charges against the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
From there, Jesus entered their community.
There was a man who had a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you grab it and pull it out?"
A man is worth more than a sheep! Therefore, the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out; it was completely like a new hand, exactly like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. Abd Allah Al-Mukhtar
Jesus was aware of all the schemes of the Pharisees. So he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
That was to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah spoke. He says,
Here is my servant, whom I choose, whom I love. I am very pleased with him. I will put my Spirit upon him. He will proclaim to the nations that everything will be alright.
He will not argue or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a leaning twig. He will not extinguish a faint flame. He will fix everything.
You have searched for "Jesus and the swallowing of the zab," and the following results were found.
A man who was controlled by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man could speak and see.
All the people were amazed. They said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow drives out demons by the prince of demons, Beelzebul."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself will be ruined; and every city or household divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By what power then do *your* people cast them out? So *they* will be your judges.
But suppose I drive out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and just take what he owns? You have to tie him up first. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
So here is what I tell you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
He who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But he who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. This man will not be forgiven, either now or in the days to come.
If you make the tree good, its fruit will be good. And if you make the tree bad, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
O nest of poisonous vipers! How can the wicked say a good thing? Your tongues speak all that is in your hearts.
A good man says good things. These come from the goodness stored up within him. An evil man says evil things. These come from the evil stored up within him.
But that's what I'm telling you. On the day of reckoning, people will be held accountable for every careless word they spoke.
With your words you are judged guilty or innocent." The miraculous verse of Greece.
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus. They said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered: "Evil and treacherous people seek a miraculous sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a large fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will be in the tomb for three days and three nights.
The men of Nineveh will rise up in the resurrection with this generation, and the men of Nineveh will stand up in judgment against this generation, because they repented at the preaching of Jonah; and behold, someone greater than Jonah is here.
The southern queen will stand at the judgment day with the people who are living now, and will prove that they are guilty. She came from a very far place to listen to the wisdom of Solomon, and now there is here one who is greater than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a person? It passes through dry areas, searching for a place to rest, but it does not find it.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." When he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and tidied.
Then the evil spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more evil than itself. And they enter and live there. And that man will be worse off than he was before. So it will be with the evil men from this day forward." "Jesus' Mother and Brothers"
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak to you."
Jesus answered: "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. He said, "This is my mother! These are my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
That very day, Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat and sat down in it. All the people stood on the beach.
He then told them many things using stories. "A farmer went out to sow his seeds," he said.
He sowed the seed on the ground. Some of it fell on the road. The birds came and ate it.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried up because they had no roots.
Another seed fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and occupied the place of the plants.
Other seed fell on good soil and produced a crop that was a hundred or sixty or thirty times more than what was planted.
Those who have ears, let them hear.
The students came to him. They asked: "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "I have been given a chance to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. It was not given to strangers."
Everyone who possesses this type of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have a lot more. And if no one has this type of knowledge, then even the little that they have will be taken away from them.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They are looking, but they don't really see. They hear, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, "You will indeed hear but never understand; you will indeed see but never perceive."
The hearts of these people have become callous. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they could see with their eyes. And hear with their ears. And understand with their hearts. And turn to the Lord, who would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and blessed are your ears because they hear.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Many prophets and righteous people have wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the farmer's story.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like the seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell on rocky places. These are the ones who hear the word and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots. So they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when there is pressure or suffering because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the word, but the anxieties of life and the false promises of riches stifle it, preventing it from bearing fruit.
"But others received the seed that fell on good ground. They are the ones who hear the word and understand it. They produce a crop, thirty or sixty or a hundredfold of what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another story to the crowds. "Here is what the Kingdom of Heaven is like," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field."
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? How then have the weeds come up?"
He said, "An enemy did this." He asked the deacon, "Do you want us to go and pull up the weeds?"
The owner said, "No, while weeding, you might uproot the wheat along with it."
Let both grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. Here is what I will tell them. First, gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it into my barn." The parables of the mustard seed and the yeast
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it becomes the largest of all the garden plants. It turns into a tree. Birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another story. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way all through the dough."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowd in parables. He did not say anything to them without a parable.
The words spoken by the Prophet were fulfilled. He said, "I will open my mouth and speak in parables. I will speak of things that were hidden from the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus interprets the story of the sticks
Then Jesus left the crowd and went into the house. His disciples came to him. They said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the son of man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds will be pulled up and burned in the fire. This is how the Day of Judgment will be.
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will remove from his kingdom everything that causes sin. They will also destroy everyone who does evil.
They will cast them into the burning oven. There the people will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear. Tales of the hidden treasure and the pearl
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and went off rejoicing greatly. Then he went and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
Again, the Kingdom of Heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found one that was extremely valuable. So he went and sold everything he had. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. It gathered all kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it to the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate between the people who did what is wrong and those who did what is right.
The wicked will be thrown into the fiery furnace. There, the wicked will cry and grind their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He told them, "Every scribe who is a student of the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out of his storeroom new treasures as well as old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom from? Where did he get this power to perform miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all of his sisters with us? So where did this man take this whole thing?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and in his own house."
There were only a few miracles there because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time, reports about Jesus had reached Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea.
He said to his companions, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John. He bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of his brother Herod Philip.
John was telling Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias' daughter danced before Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He swore to give her anything she asked for.
Her mother told her what to say. And the girl said to Herod, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very upset. But he remembered his promise and his dinner guests. So he asked one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large plate and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowd heard about this, and they followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When the evening approached, the disciples came to Him. They said, "This is a desolate place, and it is already late. Send the crowd away to go and buy food for themselves in the villages."
Jesus said, "There is no need for them to go. You give them something to eat."
They said, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
Bring them to me, he said.
Then Jesus instructed the people to sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and gave thanks. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. The disciples collected twelve baskets of leftovers.
There were about 5,000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. And he commanded them to go before him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the multitude away.
After sending them away, he went up by himself to a mountainside to pray. As evening came, he was there alone.
The ship was already a long way from the shore. She was being tossed by waves, because the wind was blowing against her.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "It's a ghost!" they cried. And they screamed in fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, "Be courageous! I am he. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, are you really you?" Peter asked. "If it is, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." And Peter, stepping out of the boat, walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. He said, "Your faith is exceedingly weak!" "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they got into the boat, the wind died down.
Then those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message throughout the neighboring countryside. People brought all their sick to Jesus.
They begged him to allow those who were only sick to touch the edge of his clothes. And everyone they touched was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked him,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered: "And why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, " 'Honor your father and your mother' " (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16). And He also said, " 'Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will be surely put to death' " (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9).
But you allow people to tell their parents: "Any help you may have received from us is a gift intended for God."
Therefore, they do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You are making the word of God void for the sake of following your own teachings.
Protesters! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
These people honor me with their words. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They know nothing but man-made rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. He said, "Listen, and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." It is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angry when they heard this?"
Jesus said, "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant. They will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
Have you not yet understood? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that enters the mouth goes to the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But that which proceeds out of the mouth, it is from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and other sexual sins. Likewise stealing, false testimony, and lying to others.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon is torturing my daughter. She is suffering greatly."
Jesus did not say a single word. His disciples approached him. They begged him, saying, "Send her away, for she is crying out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
Jesus replied, "Woman, your faith is great! What you asked for will be given." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus went there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the side of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought to him the blind and those who could not walk. They also brought the lame, the mute, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were treated. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have already been with me for three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will faint on their way home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
How many loaves of bread do you have? Jesus asked. They said: Seven, and a few small fish.
Jesus said to the multitudes to sit down on the ground.
Taking the five loaves and the fish, He thanked and broke them, and distributed them to the disciples. And the disciples passed the food to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 4,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he entered the boat. He set course for the area near Magdala.
Jesus is asked for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He replied: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today will be stormy. The sky is red and overcast." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
An evil and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; but no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
Beware, Jesus told them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils talked about this among themselves. They said, "He must be saying this because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you talking to each other about there being no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Haven't you remembered the five loaves for the five thousand? Haven't you remembered how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How can you not understand at all? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not asking them to be careful about the yeast used in bread. He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
They answered, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; and others Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter said, "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus said, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples what would happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem. There He must suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, and the scribes. He must be killed and on the third day rise from the dead to live again.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You are not thinking about the things of God, but about the things of people."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, they must deny themselves, take up their cross, and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul? Or what could a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some who are standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance was changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and his brother James up on a high mountain. There they were all alone.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face was radiant like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three shelters, one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud came over them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were terrified and fell to the ground with their faces in the dirt.
But Jesus came and touched them. He said to them, "Arise," "Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. While going down, Jesus told them what to do. He said to them, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," and he said, "Wait until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "That is true. It is supposed to be that Elijah comes and restores everything again."
But I tell you, Elijah has already come, and people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came up to the crowd, a man approached Jesus; he knelt down before him.
Lord, he said, have mercy on my son. He is shaking so much and suffering so much. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples. But they could not heal him.
O people who do not believe and are wicked! Jesus answered, "How long shall I be with you? How long shall I put up with you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the devil to leave the boy, and he departed from him. And he was healed at that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and asked, "Why could we not drive out that demon?"
He answered, "Because you have so little faith. I tell you the truth, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They came together in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. On the third day, he will rise from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived in Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. They asked him, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, He does," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" He asked. "Who among earth's rulers collects taxes and fees from them? Do they collect these from their own children or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children need not pay," Jesus said to him.
But we don't want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. You will find there the exact coin you need. Take it and present it to them as your tax and tribute.
